@doc """
Extract one successful Monad payload as a Result.
SafeBind (`=?`) calls this operation once after checking the input type.
An Err is passed to the current failure target; an Ok supplies its entire
payload to the Pattern. Nested payloads and child Patterns are not extracted
again by SafeBind.

Statement-final `?` uses the same input capability and failure target. For a
canonical Result input, it checks the Result chain until reaching Err or Unit.
An Err at any layer retains its original Error and stops the continuation;
only Ok layers ending in Unit continue. Non-Result inputs must have a direct
Unit payload. This does not add recursive decomposition to ordinary SafeBind.

The standard implementations are Result (identity), Option (None becomes
NoneError), and Either<Error, A> (Left retains its original Error). Existing
Errors keep their kind, message, Payload, location, cause, diagnostic and
stack trace. NoneError is created by Option's extract implementation.

MonadExtractor requires Monad, but is independent of MonadFail and Convert.
MonadFail receives an Error at the failure target; it does not extract inputs.
Implementing Monad alone does not provide this operation automatically.

SafeBind and statement-final `?` need no import. Qualified calls use
`MonadExtractor::extract(value)`; bare `extract(value)` requires
`import MonadExtractor`. This trait is not autoimported.

## REPL
xldr(1)> MonadExtractor::extract(Option::Some(3))
Ok(3)
xldr(2)> import MonadExtractor
xldr(3)> extract(Ok(Option::Some(3)))
Ok(Option::Some(3))

## Statement-final question
xldr(1)> checked: (Result<Result<Unit>> -> Result<Int>) = {|target| target?; Ok(7)}
xldr(2)> checked(Ok(Ok(())))
Ok(7)
xldr(3)> Result::is_err(checked(Ok(Err(NoneError()))))
True
"""
deftrait MonadExtractor
where
  Self: Monad
{
  @doc """
  Return the successful payload unchanged inside Ok, or an Error inside Err.
  The returned Result is the only layer processed by SafeBind. An existing
  Error must be retained without rebuilding it. The operation may explicitly
  generate an Error for an input failure that does not already contain one.

  ## REPL
  xldr(1)> MonadExtractor::extract(Option::Some(2))
  Ok(2)
  xldr(2)> Result::is_err(MonadExtractor::extract(Option<Int>::None))
  True
  """
  def extract(self: Self<$A>) -> Result<$A>
}
